{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Elohim spoke, etc.</b> One needs to know what Elohim spoke; also, why the declaration ״אֲנִי ה׳״ after He had already said to him, above, the mention of the Name, and said to him (Exodus 3:15): ״זֶה שְׁמִי לְעֹלָם״." "<b>Indeed,</b> since Moses spoke before an awesome and fearsome El things that are not proper conduct, especially before a great King — and this matter followed from the aspect that He had shown him a smiling face, a joyous face, inscribed in the name YHWH — therefore HaShem showed him faces of awe, which belong to the aspect of judgment, inscribed in the name Elohim. And that is what He says: ״וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֶל מֹשֶׁה״. And the construal of saying <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> is that Scripture tells that HaShem was speaking all the things stated in the passage in the aspect of the name Elohim, which are faces that terrify and cause the one spoken to to tremble." "And His saying <b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳</b> — meaning, on the path of their saying (Berakhot 30b): in the place of joy there shall be trembling — He intended to say to him: although He showed him the attribute of mercy in speaking with him, on this account He would not remove the veil of fear, and he would speak without awe of the Kingdom of Heaven. And this is your sign, that YHWH is the Elohim, for He speaks with him in awesome faces. Understand:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this path: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> — meaning, He spoke judgment with him. And what is the judgment? That He brought against him a claim that is heard. And He said ״אֲנִי ה׳״ — meaning: how do you attribute to me an evil attribute, heaven forfend, and say to me ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה״ — when I am YHWH, and my attribute is the attribute of kindness and mercy, and I am good to all? As indeed the prophet said (Lamentations 3:38): ״מִפִּי עֶלְיוֹן לֹא תֵצֵא הָרָעוֹת״ — only the good. And what He said there ״וְהַטּוֹב״ refers to the aspect of one particular good, namely that a person be righteous; HaShem decrees no such thing, but rather it is in the hand of every person to choose the good. And you will find that every time HaShem mentions the recompense of evil to evildoers, He specifies to say (I Samuel 24:14) ״כִּי מֵרְשָׁעִים יֵצֵא רֶשַׁע״, and so He says (Jeremiah 2:19) ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״, (Isaiah 64:5) ״וַתְּמוּגֵנוּ בְּיַד עֲוֹנֵינוּ״, (Job 8:4) ״וַיְשַׁלְּחֵם בְּיַד פִּשְׁעָם״. Whereas the good — (Psalms 85:13) ״ה׳ יִתֵּן הַטּוֹב״, (Psalms 145:9) ״טוֹב ה׳ לַכֹּל״. From this, should a trouble befall Israel, who knows whether they were liable for it and their sin caused it, or for the former reason — to fulfill the decree of affliction — or because of some among them who did not believe as they ought and the like. And you see that the elders drew back and did not go with Moses before Pharaoh, as they of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 5), and HaShem was displeased with them, such that from them this matter came about. How then did he let pass from his mouth words alien to him, to say ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה״?" "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this path: Since Moses said ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה, לָמָּה שְׁלַחְתָּנִי״, the answer came — corresponding to ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה״, He spoke harshly with him, for he spoke before his Elohim without proper honor; and Scripture concealed the words for the sake of Moses' honor. Or it may be that He said to him that He would call him to account for this at the time of judgment. And corresponding to ״לָמָּה שְׁלַחְתָּנִי״, He answered ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳״ — meaning: I am compassionate; therefore I could not bear to see Israel in distress until the appointed end, and I anticipated sending you before the arrival of the end, to lighten from upon Israel the trouble of the exile. And this answer is valid only after He prefaced by saying to him ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ — for immediately, at once, He will perform wonders upon Pharaoh with wondrous plagues, and forthwith the servitude will cease." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 3:6) in the verse ״מוֹצִיא אֲסִירִים בַּכּוֹשָׁרוֹת״ — meaning, כִי (weeping) for Egypt and שִׁירוֹת (songs) for Israel — and these are two attributes: the attribute of judgment and the attribute of mercy. Therefore the verse says: corresponding to what He prepared to judge the Egyptians, it says <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b>, which is the aspect of judgment; and corresponding to what He wished to bestow good upon Israel, it says <b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳</b>. And included in this is a hint to him that the great plagues prepared to come upon the Egyptians — even though they would come in a general way upon all of Egypt, and Israel were placed right there among them, and it is a known thing that the destroyer does not distinguish but ravages everything, and especially with the coming of the plague of darkness, reason dictates that surely it would darken in a general way for all inhabitants of the land — therefore He said that the array of judgments alluded to in the name Elohim, which YHWH prepared to make the Egyptians drink, would be governed by the attribute of mercy for the sake of Israel; and this is what He said: ״אֲנִי ה׳״. And so too it says (Exodus 10:23) ״וּלְכָל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל הָיָה אוֹר״, (Exodus 9:6) ״וּמִמִּקְנֵה יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא מֵת״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 33:3) that the wicked turn the attribute of mercy into the attribute of judgment — and this itself is what the verse says here: for even the attribute of mercy agreed to execute judgment upon Egypt. And this is the sense of the verse: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b>, which is an appellation for judgment, <b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו</b> etc. — I make known to you that this attribute of judgment is from the name הוי״ה, for even the mercy was turned upon them into judgment." "<b>Further,</b> it also hints, concerning Israel, according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 33:2): happy are the righteous, who turn the attribute of judgment into the attribute of mercy. And this is what it says: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים וַיֹּאמֶר</b> etc. — meaning, this attribute of judgment that you see has become mercy, denoted by the name הוי״ה. And from this Moses would establish in his mind that the decree of judgment for the evil of Pharaoh was sealed even with the agreement of mercy, and the decree of judgment for the good of Israel was sealed even through the attribute of judgment; and by this his hands would be strengthened in his mission." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to answer concerning what Moses complained — why YHWH did not act with zeal. And He said, I am Elohim; and this is what it says: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> — meaning, He spoke to him as Elohim, who brings all to trial, and Pharaoh He would judge with full judgment. And the reason He did not hurry — ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳״ — and this name contains within it the attributes known as the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy, one of which is אֶרֶךְ אַפַּיִם (slow to anger), who does not hasten to punish the wicked for the deeds of their hands." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this line: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> — and this attribute of judgment came against the emissary, who is Moses, who spoke before HaShem not according to proper conduct; but against those being sent to them, who are Israel — ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳״." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this line: <b>and Elohim spoke to Moses</b> — He sat upon him in judgment; ״and He said to him, I am YHWH״ — meaning, even though I must judge you, toward you I conduct the attribute of mercy. And this is why He was precise in saying ״to him, I am YHWH״ — and the reason is along the lines of what the Sages of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 17b): to whom does He bear iniquity? — to him who passes over transgression. And it is well known that Moses was exceedingly humble and bore forbearance beyond all created beings; therefore YHWH conducted Himself with him in this measure and bore his iniquity, though He was warranted to come upon him in judgment — and this is His saying ״I am YHWH,״ alluding to the attribute of ״bearing iniquity,״ so that there is no judgment upon him." "<b>Further</b>, YHWH hinted at the sod of His saying ״YHWH, He is Elohim״ (I Kings 18:39), and this is His saying ״and Elohim spoke… and He said… I am YHWH״ — and this is the revelation of the unification of YHWH, which YHWH revealed, the sod of it, to His servant the prophet, faithful in His house."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:41.826613142Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}